  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="7c8dc83b-e64e-5bd1-b3d1-7716785af09c" activityNameId="8f90d9e1-91ec-5d1c-9510-76088a71b994" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Copper, blister-copper, at primary smelter</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Copper, blister-copper, at primary smelter
The product "blister copper" is a intermediary product from primary copper production used uniquely in the secondary copper production where apart from copper scrap 10% of the feed consists of blister copper, i. e. raw copper after converting with a average Cu content of 98%.;
UUID: d5b55ba3-2d90-30aa-b7a2-25efb6602529
The module includes the same steps as the primary copper production without the refining.
Location: This module is valid for the whole corresponding region. Some agents used like lime or sodium cyanide originate from data sets valid for Switzerland or Europe.
Technology: The ore is pre-treated and reduced according to the country specific mix of process alternatives: reverberatory furnace 6.2%; flash smelting furnaces 76%; other 17.8%. An overall abatement for sulphur dioxide of 95% was estimated.
Time period: undefined
Production volume: 2.3 million tonnes in Europe in 1994 , SX-EW copper excluded
Sampling: literature 
Extrapolations: see Geography and Technology
Uncertainty adjustments: none
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_d5b55ba3-2d90-30aa-b7a2-25efb6602529.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
      <geography geographyId="20a9bf48-6cea-59b4-ad08-000cb981f2d0" geographyContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83">
        <shortname xml:lang="en">RER</shortname>
        <comment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Location: This module is valid for the whole corresponding region. Some agents used like lime or sodium cyanide originate from data sets valid for Switzerland or Europe.</text>
